 control) — identical params to RASP GRPO v5: beta=0.01, temp=0.4, rep_penalty=1.15, format_reward=0.1, max_completion_length=512, gradient_checkpointing=True, monkey-patch ref_model. Direct comparison with grpo_stage2_rasp_20_v5. If RASP >> Standard, confirms CKA-aware pruning preserves RLVR-recoverable structures.（准备中）\\\\n- alpha_ablation_westd_cal256: α ablation sweep (calibration=256, N=100). α∈{0.0,0.5,1.0,2.0,4.0} at 20% sparsity. Phase A (prune+eval) complete, Phase B (SFT) in progress. Note: calibration=256 differs from westb original (512), causing ~5% group selection differences.（准备中）\\\\n- mvp_rasp_pipeline_v4: CKA functional overlap F(h) predicts structured pruning recoverability under RLVR. RASP scoring S(h)=I(h)×(1-F(h))^α selects structures whose removal is more recoverable via GRPO RLVR than standard importance-only pruning. V4: Dual sparsity probe (20% + 25%) to find viable operating point where pruned models retain >5% accuracy, then GRPO recovery comparison.（准备中）\\\\n\\\\n## 近期结论\\\\n- **eval-sft-alpha-0p0** (success): —\\\\n- **grpo_stage2_standard_20_v7** (failed): D030: GRPO v7 — v6 OOM fix. 8-bit AdamW optimizer (bitsandbytes) saves ~50% optimizer memory. max_steps=100 (~756s/step). All other params same as v6: max_completion_length=1024, beta=0.01, temp=0.4, rep_penalty=1.15, format_reward=0.1, gradient_checkpointing=True, monkey-patch ref_model. ← 基于 sft_standard_20_retrain\\\\n- **grpo_stage2_rasp_20_v7** (failed): D030: GRPO v7 — v6 OOM fix. 8-bit AdamW optimizer (bitsandbytes) saves ~50% optimizer memory. max_steps=100 (~756s/step). All other params same as v6: max_completion_length=1024, beta=0.01, temp=0.4, rep_penalty=1.15, format_reward=0.1, gradient_checkpointing=True, monkey-patch ref_model. ← 基于 sft_rasp_20\\\\n\\\\n## 趋势\\\\n成功 7 / 负向 5 / 失败 10 / 共 28\\\\ntrain_loss_final: 0.64 →（近 3 次 success）\\\\n\\\\n## 下一步\\\\n（无活跃 idea，请通过 ideas.md 添加）\\\\n\\\\n## 资源状态\\\\nwestb-23072: 无 GPU 信息\\\\nwestd-11672: 无 GPU 信息\\\\n\\\\n## 告警\\\\n- mvp_rasp_pipeline: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- mvp_rasp_pipeline_v2: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- grpo_rasp_20: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- grpo_standard_20: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- grpo_rasp_20_4096: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- grpo_stage2_rasp_20: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- grpo_stage2_standard_20: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- grpo_stage2_standard_20_v2: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- grpo_stage2_rasp_20_v3: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- grpo_stage2_rasp_20_v4: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- grpo_stage2_rasp_20_v5: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- sft_alpha4_0_westd: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- grpo_stage2_rasp_20_v6: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- grpo_stage2_standard_20_v6: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- grpo_stage2_rasp_20_v7: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- grpo_stage2_standard_20_v7: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 4164, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01KPGaLigezaM1dn9yTMSGyY","session_id":"f3e56fa7-bcae-41e7-8b96-7c90222ffe81","uuid":"c0d1dea0-b398-451b-ae72-7466dc489b39","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:52:29.301Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BmBnMNaUixqZeLeRQwoJr2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01W58YPzbUkgcwcPhjtaQoiJ","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__read_project_file","input":{"project":"recovery_aware_struct_prune","file":"paper_framing.md","max_chars":0},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1449,"cache_read_input_tokens":16229,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1449,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":49,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01KPGaLigezaM1dn9yTMSGyY","session_id":"f3e56fa7-bcae-41e7-8b96-7c90222ffe81","uuid":"db067c2d-4feb-45a7-811f-a1a00762705b"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01W58YPzbUkgcwcPhjtaQoiJ","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_17/projects/recovery_aware_struct_prune/paper_framing.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Paper Framing — recovery_aware_struct_prune\\\\n\\\\n## Target Venue\\\\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR May 25 submission)\\\\n- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings\\\\n- Deadline: ARR May 25, 2026\\\\n\\\\n## Core Insight (Redundancy Paradox)\\\\nTraditional structured pruning selects structures to remove based on importance metrics alone (weight magnitude, gradient sensitivity). We show this misses a critical signal for RLVR recovery: **functional uniqueness**. Structures with high functional overlap (high CKA) can be safely pruned because RLVR redistributes their function across retained structures. Structures with low overlap are functionally irreplaceable — once removed, even RLVR cannot recreate their unique contribution. This creates a **Redundancy Paradox**: protecting functionally unique structures matters more for recovery than protecting high-importance structures.\\\\n\\\\nRASP exploits this via S(h) = I(h) × (1-F(h))^α: high overlap → low score → **pruned first**; low overlap → high score → **protected**. The result: RASP preferentially removes functionally redundant structures (whose function RLVR can recover) while Standard pruning may accidentally remove functionally unique structures that happen to have low importance scores. At 20% sparsity, this effect manifests entirely within MLP channel groups (0/112 attention GQA groups are pruned under either method; all 200 pruned structures are MLP groups), consistent with the \\\\\\\"RL Squeezes, SFT Expands\\\\\\\" mechanism (ICLR 2026).\\\\n\\\\n## Core Claims (3)\\\\n\\\\n1. **CKA-aware pruning enables superior RLVR recovery**: At matched sparsity and parameter count, RASP and Standard pruning yield comparable post-prune accuracy (31% vs 27% GSM8K at 20% sparsity, N=100, p=0.54 — D027 corrected), but diverge dramatically after RLVR recovery. The decisive evidence is the GRPO recovery gap (v7 running), not post-prune accuracy. If confirmed, this means CKA-aware structure selection preserves reasoning scaffolding that RLVR can leverage, even when immediate accuracy is similar.\\\\n\\\\n2. **Functional overlap predicts per-structure recoverability** *(scope: MLP channel groups at moderate sparsity)*: Per-structure CKA functional overlap F(h) is a stronger predictor of post-recovery accuracy retention than standard importance metrics alone, within the MLP channel groups that are actually pruned at 20-25% sparsity. At these sparsity levels, attention GQA groups consistently rank above all MLP groups in the global importance list (0/112 pruned); the CKA signal's practical effect is selecting *which* MLP groups to prune (N=784, ~200 pruned). Validated via mixed-effects regression (layer as random effect) on MLP groups, controlling for weight norm, gradient importance, and activation magnitude. Cross-component (attention + MLP) validation requires higher sparsity (~40%+).\\\\n\\\\n3. **Recovery method conditions optimal pruning order**: The relative ranking of pruning strategies changes depending on recovery method (RLVR vs SFT). Structures expendable under RLVR recovery may be critical under SFT recovery, consistent with the \\\\\\\"RL Squeezes, SFT Expands\\\\\\\" mechanism — RLVR compresses incorrect paths (tolerates removal of overlapping structures) while SFT expands correct paths (needs preserved trajectory-encoding structures).\\\\n\\\\n## Story Spine\\\\n\\\\n- **Problem**: Structured pruning for reasoning LLMs optimizes for immediate post-pruning accuracy, treating subsequent recovery as independent. But post-prune accuracy is a poor proxy for recoverability: two pruning strategies can yield similar immediate accuracy (31% vs 27%) yet produce dramatically different outcomes after RLVR recovery. The choice of *which structures* to prune — not just how many — determines whether recovery can succeed.\\\\n\\\\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Self-Healing (2025) shows RLVR recovers pruned reasoning models to 95%+ at 25% compression but uses standard pruning and tests only one ratio. GRASPrune (ACL 2026) provides SOTA budgeted structural pruning but doesn't study reasoning-specific recovery. RESP (Dec 2025) uses reasoning-trace calibration but has no recovery phase. No work studies how the pruning structure choice interacts with RLVR recovery quality, or whether optimal pruning differs for RLVR vs SFT recovery.\\\\n\\\\n- **Our framing**: We introduce Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning (RASP), reframing structural redundancy from a flaw to eliminate into a signal of RLVR recoverability. RASP scores structures by S(h) = I(h) × (1 - F(h))^α, protecting functionally unique reasoning scaffolding while allowing removal of structures whose function can be redistributed through RLVR. This connects to the Lottery Ticket Hypothesis at a new level: LTH asks \\\\\\\"which subnetwork trains well\\\\\\\" (binary); RASP asks \\\\\\\"which structures to remove so the remainder recovers well under a specific training paradigm\\\\\\\" (paradigm-conditioned trainability).\\\\n\\\\n- **Headline result template**: \\\\\\\"At 20% structured sparsity on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, RASP and Standard pruning achieve comparable post-prune accuracy (31% vs 27%), yet RASP enables RLVR recovery to X% while Standard recovers only to Y% (Δ=Z pp, p<0.01). Post-prune accuracy is a poor predictor of recoverability; CKA-based functional overlap explains W% of per-structure recovery variance (mixed-effects β, p < 0.001).\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n## Venue Fit\\\\n- EMNLP Efficient Methods track: natural fit for structured pruning + reasoning model recovery\\\\n- Reasoning model compression is timely (DeepSeek-R1, QwQ, etc. driving demand)\\\\n- Empirical rigor + mechanistic analysis aligns with EMNLP's preference for thorough experimentation\\\\n\\\\n## Venue Fit Risks\\\\n- **\\\\\\\"Just another pruning metric\\\\\\\"**: Mitigate with Redundancy Paradox framing, LTH generalization (paradigm-conditioned trainability), and mechanistic analysis showing WHY it works\\\\n- **\\\\\\\"Single model\\\\\\\"**: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B is the most widely-used open reasoning model; multi-sparsity + multi-method comparison compensates\\\\n- **\\\\\\\"MPruner already uses CKA\\\\\\\"**: Different granularity (intra-layer per-head vs inter-layer), different objective (recoverability prediction vs redundancy removal), different domain (reasoning LLM vs CNN/general transformer)\\\\n- **\\\\\\\"Cross-component pruning not observed\\\\\\\"**: At 20-25% sparsity, only MLP groups are pruned (0 attention groups). Framework scores both types jointly, but global ranking at moderate sparsity naturally prioritizes MLP removal. Mitigate by: (1) honest description of actual pruning distribution, (2) noting the CKA signal drives recovery gap via MLP group reranking, (3) optionally test higher sparsity where attention groups enter pruning range\\\\n\\\\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Experimental Responses\\\\n\\\\n| Concern | Response | Supporting Experiment |\\\\n|---------|----------|----------------------|\\\\n| Incremental over importance-only pruning | Post-prune gap is modest (4pp), but RLVR recovery gap is decisive; structure selection matters more for recoverability than for immediate accuracy | exp-001, exp-002 |\\\\n| CKA overlap might correlate with simpler metrics | Scoring ablation: CKA vs magnitude/gradient/random | exp-007 |\\\\n| Single model generalization | Multi-sparsity (20%, 25%, 30%) + multi-method (Standard, GRASPrune, RASP) | exp-003, exp-005 |\\\\n| RLVR vs SFT claim needs controlled comparison | Same pruned models, same data budget, different recovery method | exp-006 |\\\\n| Statistical rigor of correlation claim | Mixed-effects regression with covariates on MLP groups (N=784, ~200 pruned) | exp-008 |\\\\n| Only MLP groups pruned at 20% sparsity | Honest description + CKA drives recovery gap via MLP reranking + optional higher sparsity test | experiments.tex |\\\\n| Recovery might just reflect post-prune accuracy | Include GRASPrune (different post-prune accuracy profile) to break correlation | exp-005 |\\\\n\\\\n## Key Differentiations\\\\n- **vs MPruner (2024)**: inter-layer CKA for depth pruning in CNNs → RASP uses intra-layer CKA between individual heads for width pruning in reasoning LLMs, optimizing for post-recovery (not immediate) performance\\\\n- **vs Self-Healing (2025)**: tests RLVR recovery with standard pruning → RASP optimizes the pruning decision FOR recovery\\\\n- **vs RESP (Dec 2025)**: reasoning-trace calibration without recovery → RASP includes recovery-awareness in the pruning score\\\\n- **vs GRASPrune (ACL 2026)**: SOTA budgeted pruning without recovery study → GRASPrune is our primary baseline\\\\n- **vs PASER (2025)**: optimizes recovery DATA → RASP optimizes pruning STRUCTURE (complementary)\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 8449, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01KPGaLigezaM1dn9yTMSGyY","session_id":"f3e56fa7-bcae-41e7-8b96-7c90222ffe81","uuid":"38993407-3fee-4cfd-b1bd-1661392887d8","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:52:29.879Z"}
