{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KPGaLigezaM1dn9yTMSGyY","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me now produce the formal review, carefully distinguishing between what has been actually run versus what is \"imagined\" (dream) data.\n\nBased on my thorough analysis of the paper materials, project logs, decision history, and experiment status, here is my review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning for Reasoning Language Models\n\n**Reviewer R1 — Focus: Methods Soundness & Experimental Rigor**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper proposes RASP, a structured pruning method that integrates intra-layer CKA-based functional overlap into the pruning score to optimize for post-RLVR recoverability rather than immediate post-pruning accuracy. The scoring function S(h) = I(h) x (1-F(h))^alpha penalizes redundant structures, making them candidates for removal under the hypothesis that RLVR can redistribute their function. On DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B at 20% sparsity, the paper claims a 17pp post-GRPO advantage over standard pruning (70% vs 53% GSM8K), a significant pruning-by-recovery interaction (25pp, p=0.0008), and that CKA overlap is the strongest predictor of per-structure recoverability (beta=0.38, partial R^2=0.22).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n**S1. Novel and well-motivated research question.** The idea that pruning and recovery are coupled optimization problems is genuinely interesting. The \"Redundancy Paradox\" framing --- that redundancy is not merely expendable but is the resource RLVR exploits --- is intellectually compelling and, if validated, would change how the community thinks about pruning for reasoning models.\n\n**S2. Well-designed 2x2 factorial analysis (Claim 3).** The pruning x recovery interaction design (Table 4) is the strongest conceptual contribution. If the 25pp interaction holds (main effects non-significant, interaction significant at p=0.0008), this is a clean and convincing demonstration that optimal pruning depends on recovery paradigm. This is the kind of analysis that elevates a paper above \"yet another pruning metric.\"\n\n**S3. Comprehensive experiment design covering 18 experiments.** The planned experiment matrix is thorough: multiple baselines (Standard, GRASPrune, Bonsai, Random, Unstructured), multi-sparsity (20/25/30%), OOD evaluation (MATH-500, HumanEval), scoring ablation, alpha sensitivity, multi-seed robustness, CKA reliability, inference benchmarks. If all were faithfully executed, this would be unusually thorough for the pruning literature.\n\n**S4. GRASPrune as a confound-breaking baseline (Table 8).** The inclusion of GRASPrune --- which has low post-prune accuracy (10%) but positive GRPO delta (+3pp) while Standard has higher post-prune accuracy (27%) but negative GRPO delta (-11pp) --- breaks the naive \"recovery is just a function of post-prune accuracy\" confound. This is a well-chosen control.\n\n**S5. Mixed-effects regression with appropriate random effects (Table 5).** Using layer as a random effect for the N=784 MLP group regression is methodologically appropriate. Reporting marginal R^2, conditional R^2, ICC, VIF, and controlling for competing predictors (weight norm, gradient importance, activation magnitude) shows statistical maturity.\n\n**S6. Honest scope limitation of CKA claims to MLP groups.** The paper correctly notes that at 20-25% sparsity, 0/112 attention GQA groups are pruned, and Claim 2's scope is limited to MLP channel groups. This transparency is appreciated.\n\n**S7. Thorough CKA reliability analysis.** Split-half rho=0.93, calibration size sensitivity (3pp range across 256/512/1024), and pruned-set overlap (86-95%) provide evidence that the CKA scoring is a stable measurement, not an artifact of a particular calibration sample.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1 [Major]. The GRPO results --- the entire evidential basis for Claims 1 and 3 --- appear to be imagined/projected, not actually obtained.** This is the single most critical issue. The project's internal records (ROADMAP.md, decisions.yaml, report.md) reveal that as of the paper submission date (2026-05-15), GRPO v8 is still running (ETA 2026-05-16 11:30 UTC). Versions v1 through v7 all failed due to engineering issues (truncation, collapse, OOM, disk full, silent death). The actual paper LaTeX (experiments.tex) contains \"[TBD: GRPO results]\" placeholders throughout. The \"dream report\" (best-case scenario) produces the numbers reported in the paper summary (70% vs 53%, interaction 25pp, etc.), but these are explicitly labeled as \"imagined_experiments\" in the project files. The actual confirmed results are only: post-prune RASP 31% vs Standard 27% (4pp, initially non-significant at N=100), post-SFT Standard 64% > RASP 57%. Every number in Tables 1, 3, 4, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12 that involves GRPO is projected. If GRPO v8 fails (as v1-v7 did), the paper has no viable Claim 1 or Claim 3.\n\n**W2 [Major]. Single model, single architecture.** All experiments use DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B exclusively. This is a distilled 7B model from one family (Qwen2.5). There is no validation on any other model (LLaMA, Mistral, Gemma), any other scale (1B, 14B, 32B), or even the non-distilled version of the same model. The \"Redundancy Paradox\" is stated as a general principle about RLVR recovery, but evidence comes from one model at one scale. For a top venue, this is insufficient to support the generality of the framing.\n\n**W3 [Major]. Evaluation is overwhelmingly GSM8K-centric.** GSM8K is used for calibration, SFT training, GRPO training, and primary evaluation. The model is trained and evaluated on the same benchmark distribution. OOD evaluation (MATH-500, HumanEval) is reported but: (a) the GRPO-based numbers for OOD appear to be imagined (same concern as W1), and (b) only post-pruning OOD is actually testable without GRPO completion. The recovery pipeline is GSM8K-specific (SFT on GSM8K train, GRPO on GSM8K train), raising the question of whether RASP's advantage is specific to GSM8K-style arithmetic reasoning.\n\n**W4 [Major]. The alpha ablation reveals a troubling pattern (Table 7).** The monotonically increasing curve (alpha=0: 25%, alpha=0.25: 27%, alpha=0.5: 29%, alpha=1.0: 31%, alpha=2.0: 35%) with no peak strongly suggests that the (1-F(h))^alpha term is simply acting as a regularizer that spreads pruning more evenly across layers, not specifically that CKA overlap captures \"functional redundancy\" for RLVR. Any monotonic reweighting that disperses pruning would produce a similar curve. The absence of a non-monotonic relationship (i.e., a point where too much CKA emphasis hurts) weakens the mechanistic interpretation. The paper needs to control for this confound --- e.g., by comparing against a random dispersion score (1-R(h))^alpha where R(h) is random or layer-dependent but not CKA.\n\n**W5 [Major]. Potential circularity in the CKA-recoverability regression (Claim 2).** The regression in Table 5 predicts \"per-structure recoverability\" from CKA overlap. But recoverability is defined as post-recovery accuracy, and RASP itself uses CKA to decide which structures to prune. If the pruning decision is CKA-driven, and the dependent variable is accuracy after pruning a CKA-selected set, the predictor and outcome are mechanically coupled. The paper needs to establish this correlation on models pruned by a CKA-independent method (e.g., Standard-pruned or randomly-pruned models) to demonstrate that CKA overlap predicts recoverability independently of the pruning criterion.\n\n**W6 [Minor]. The per-layer capacity floor (40%) is a significant design choice that is not ablated.** The paper mentions this floor was added because \"unconstrained global ranking at 20% sparsity concentrates pruning in layers with uniformly high functional overlap, collapsing those layers' capacity.\" This floor may be doing much of the heavy lifting --- RASP might primarily work by preventing layer collapse rather than through CKA-based selection per se. The paper mentions a planned ablation over tau in {0%, 20%, 40%, 60%} but this appears as \"[TBD]\" in the experiments section.\n\n**W7 [Minor]. Multi-seed analysis only covers post-prune, not the full pipeline.** Table 2 reports 5-seed variance for post-prune accuracy (4.5 +/- 0.8 pp gap, p=0.0002), but the critical claim is about post-GRPO recovery. GRPO training is stochastic and adds substantial variance. A 5-seed analysis of the full pipeline (prune -> SFT -> GRPO -> eval) would be far more convincing but is absent.\n\n**W8 [Minor]. Post-prune accuracy gap is small and originally mischaracterized.** The project history reveals that the original \"RASP 29% vs Standard 1%\" result (a 29x advantage) was due to an eval bug (max_new_tokens=10 truncation). After correction, the gap is 31% vs 27% (4pp), not significant at N=100. The paper's introduction still contains the erroneous \"29% vs 1%\" claim (line 28 of introduction.tex). While the corrected value appears in the dream paper, the actual LaTeX being prepared contains unresolved contradictions.\n\n**W9 [Minor]. GRPO hyperparameter sensitivity not explored.** The paper reports one GRPO configuration (beta=0.01, temp=0.4, rep_penalty=1.15, 100 steps). But the project logs show that GRPO behavior was extremely fragile: v1-v7 failed with different hyperparameter combinations. A natural question is whether Standard-pruned models might recover under different GRPO settings (e.g., lower temperature, more steps, different learning rate). The comparison may be sensitive to these choices.\n\n**W10 [Minor]. The KL divergence argument (Table 3) is post-hoc.** The paper argues that RASP's higher KL (4.1 vs 1.8) indicates \"greater policy movement\" and active exploration. But higher KL could equally indicate instability or catastrophic forgetting. Without decomposing what the KL change represents (which reasoning paths were compressed vs. expanded), this is an unfalsifiable narrative.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Questions to Authors\n\n**Q1.** Can you confirm which results in the paper are from actually completed experiments versus projected/imagined numbers? Specifically, have GRPO recovery experiments been completed for any configuration?\n\n**Q2.** The alpha ablation shows a monotonically increasing curve. Have you tested whether a control score of the form S(h) = I(h) x (1-D(h))^alpha, where D(h) is a dispersion term unrelated to CKA (e.g., inverse layer concentration), produces a similar monotonic improvement? This would distinguish the \"CKA captures functional redundancy\" story from the \"any dispersive reweighting helps\" story.\n\n**Q3.** For the mixed-effects regression (Table 5), was the recoverability dependent variable measured on RASP-pruned models, Standard-pruned models, or both? If only on RASP-pruned models, how do you address the circularity concern (predictor and selection criterion are the same)?\n\n**Q4.** The per-layer capacity floor of 40% was introduced to fix a collapse failure mode. What is the post-prune accuracy of RASP without this floor? If the floor alone accounts for most of the 4pp post-prune advantage, the contribution of CKA overlap scoring is marginal.\n\n**Q5.** Why was alpha=1.0 selected as the default when alpha=2.0 performs better (35% vs 31%)? If the answer is \"conservative choice,\" this undermines the paper's own claim that CKA emphasis is beneficial.\n\n**Q6.** The paper argues that Standard pruning \"accidentally removes functionally unique structures.\" Can you quantify this? How many of the 200 structures removed by Standard vs RASP are classified as \"functionally unique\" (e.g., bottom quartile of F(h))?\n\n**Q7.** You report that Standard achieves 64% post-SFT versus RASP's 57%. Is this 7pp gap significant? If so, an alternative interpretation is that Standard preserves a better architecture for learning and GRPO simply fails due to hyperparameter fragility rather than structural composition.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Missing Experiments or Controls\n\n**E1.** The capacity floor ablation (tau in {0%, 20%, 40%, 60%}) is listed as TBD. This is essential to isolate the CKA contribution from the floor contribution.\n\n**E2.** A dispersion control baseline (non-CKA-based reweighting that prevents layer concentration) is needed to test whether CKA overlap specifically, or any dispersive penalty, drives the improvement.\n\n**E3.** Multi-seed evaluation of the full pipeline (including GRPO) is missing. Post-prune multi-seed is necessary but not sufficient.\n\n**E4.** A second model family (e.g., LLaMA-3.1-8B with reasoning recovery, or Mistral-7B-Instruct) would substantially strengthen generalization claims.\n\n**E5.** Correlation between I(h) and F(h) is not reported. If high-importance structures tend to have high overlap, the (1-F)^alpha term may be approximately equivalent to a different importance normalization, not a fundamentally new signal.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Statistical Rigor Assessment\n\nThe paper demonstrates awareness of statistical methodology (mixed-effects regression, bootstrap CIs, paired t-tests, Fisher's exact test, Cohen's h). The regression design in Table 5 is appropriate if the circularity concern (W5) is addressed. The 2x2 interaction test is well-specified.\n\nHowever, there are significant gaps:\n\n- The primary claim (17pp GRPO gap) lacks any significance test in the actual paper --- bootstrap CIs are reported only in the dream version.\n- N=100 quick evaluations are used for several comparisons where N=1319 is needed (the paper acknowledges this).\n- The alpha ablation lacks significance tests between adjacent values. With N=100 and overlapping CIs, most pairwise differences (e.g., 29% vs 31%) are non-significant.\n- The interaction ANOVA (Table 4) is a 2x2 design with N=4 cell-level observations (one model per cell). The degrees of freedom for the interaction test are not reported. If this is an observation-level test (comparing N=1319 predictions per cell), the independence assumption is violated since all predictions come from the same model. If it is a model-level test, N=4 is inadequate.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Reproducibility Concerns\n\n**R1.** The codebase exists and appears functional (cka_overlap.py, rasp_scoring.py, prune.py, train_grpo.py, eval_gsm8k.py). Core algorithmic components are reproducible.\n\n**R2.** The GRPO recovery stage is extremely fragile. Eight versions were attempted, seven failed for engineering reasons. Reproducing the GRPO recovery results on different hardware would likely require substantial hyperparameter tuning not documented in the paper.\n\n**R3.** The per-layer capacity floor (40%) was introduced to fix a specific failure mode. The paper presents it as a minor engineering choice, but the project logs suggest it is critical for avoiding catastrophic collapse. Its interaction with the CKA scoring is not characterized.\n\n**R4.** The calibration sensitivity (D022) reveals that the model is at a \"fragile threshold\" at 20% sparsity, where small changes in calibration samples (256 vs 512) cause large accuracy swings. This fragility makes reproduction on different calibration sets potentially unstable.\n\n**R5.** Hyperparameters for GRPO evolved through 8 iterations of failure-driven tuning. The final configuration (v8: 8-bit AdamW, max_completion_length=1024, beta=0.01, temp=0.4, rep_penalty=1.15, format_reward=0.1, gradient_checkpointing=True) was arrived at through extensive debugging, not principled selection. This makes the comparison with Standard potentially unfair if Standard requires a different GRPO configuration.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Overall Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n\n**Justification.** The research question is genuinely novel and the experimental design (if fully executed) would be strong. The \"Redundancy Paradox\" framing and the 2x2 pruning-by-recovery interaction are conceptual contributions that would interest the community. However, several issues prevent acceptance:\n\n1. The headline results (Tables 1, 3, 4, and most others involving GRPO) appear to be projected rather than obtained. The core experiments were still running at submission time, with 7/8 prior attempts having failed. For a top venue, results must be actual, not imagined.\n\n2. Single model/single architecture severely limits the generalizability of claims stated as general principles about RLVR and structured pruning.\n\n3. The alpha ablation's monotonic pattern raises a serious confound: CKA may be acting as a generic dispersion regularizer rather than capturing \"functional redundancy\" per se. Without a dispersion control, the mechanistic narrative is underdetermined.\n\n4. The circularity concern in Claim 2 (CKA predicts recoverability when CKA is used to select pruning targets) is not adequately addressed.\n\nIf the GRPO results are genuine and the authors can address the dispersion confound and circularity concerns, this paper could merit a higher score (4-5). As submitted, the evidentiary basis is insufficient.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Confidence: 4/5\n\nI have carefully examined the paper text, project files, experiment logs, decision history, and codebase. I am confident in my assessment of what has been actually verified versus projected. The one uncertainty is whether GRPO v8 completed successfully between my review and the final submission --- if it did and the numbers match projections, the severity of W1 decreases, though the remaining weaknesses (W2-W5) stand independently."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a853eb05b34e76496 (use SendMessage with to: 'a853eb05b34e76496' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 110903\ntool_uses: 27\nduration_ms: 172765</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f3e56fa7-bcae-41e7-8b96-7c90222ffe81","uuid":"f1f08fa7-ee64-4dd3-8dcf-ab5857bcefbf","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:55:15.201Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus: **Methods soundness** and **Experimental rigor**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning: Optimizing What to Remove for How You Recover\n\n**Core idea**: Structured pruning of reasoning LLMs should account for the recovery method (SFT vs RLVR). The paper introduces RASP, which scores structures by S(h) = I(h) × (1−F(h))^α, where F(h) is functional overlap via linear CKA. RASP removes high-overlap (redundant) structures, preserving functionally unique ones that RLVR needs as \"anchors\" for exploration.\n\n**Model**: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B at 20% structured sparsity (also 25%, 30%).\n\n**Three Claims**:\n1. RASP achieves 84.1% recovery vs 63.7% for standard pruning (17pp gap, p<0.001)\n2. Per-structure CKA overlap is the strongest predictor of recoverability (β=0.38, partial R²=0.22, p<0.001)\n3. 25pp pruning×recovery interaction (p=0.0008): standard beats RASP under SFT (66% vs 58%) but RASP dominates under RLVR (70% vs 53%)\n\n## Key Tables from Paper\n\n**Table 1 (Main Results, 20% sparsity, GSM8K N=1319)**:\n| Method | Post-Prune | +SFT | +GRPO | Recovery |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Baseline | 83.2 | — | — | — |\n| RASP | 31.0 | 57 | 70.0 | 84.1% |\n| Standard | 27.0 | 64 | 53.0 | 63.7% |\n| Bonsai | 24.0 | 50 | 47.0 | 56.5% |\n| GRASPrune | 10.0 | 35 | 38.0 | 45.7% |\n\n**Table 2 (5-seed post-prune, 20% sparsity)**:\nMean±Std: RASP 31.1±1.1, Standard 26.6±0.7, Gap 4.5±0.8, paired t p=0.0002, pruned-set overlap 87-93%.\n\n**Table 3 (SFT→GRPO dynamics)**:\n| Method | Post-Prune | Post-SFT | Post-GRPO | GRPO Δ | KL |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| RASP | 31 | 57 | 70 | +13 | 4.1 |\n| Standard | 27 | 64 | 53 | −11 | 1.8 |\n\n**Table 4 (2×2 Interaction)**:\n| Pruning | SFT-Only | SFT+GRPO | GRPO Marginal |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| RASP | 58 | 70 | +12 |\n| Standard | 66 | 53 | −13 |\n| RASP Advantage | −8 | +17 | — |\nMain effects non-significant; interaction 25pp, p=0.0008.\n\n**Table 5 (Mixed-effects regression, N=784 MLP groups, 28 layers random effect)**:\n| Predictor | β (std) | SE | p | Partial R² |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| F(h) CKA overlap | 0.38 | 0.055 | <0.001 | 0.22 |\n| I(h) gradient | 0.12 | 0.052 | 0.019 | 0.04 |\n| Weight norm | 0.07 | 0.050 | 0.15 | 0.01 |\n| Activation mag. | 0.04 | 0.050 | 0.42 | <0.01 |\nMarginal R²=0.31, Conditional R²=0.49, ICC=0.18.\n\n**Table 6 (Scoring ablation, post-prune)**:\nRASP(full) 31.0 > CKA-only 29.0 > Gradient-only 27.0 > Magnitude 25.0 > Random 18.0\n\n**Table 7 (α sensitivity, post-prune)**:\nα=0: 25%, α=0.25: 27%, α=0.5: 29%, α=1.0: 31%, α=2.0: 35%. Monotonically increasing, no peak.\n\n**Table 8 (GRASPrune breaks linear assumption)**:\nGRASPrune post-prune 10% but GRPO Δ=+3pp (vs Standard's −11pp at 27% post-prune).\n\n**Table 9 (Multi-sparsity)**:\n20%: RASP 70 vs Standard 53 (gap 17pp). 25%: RASP 55 vs Standard 32 (gap 23pp). 30%: both near-collapse.\n\n**Table 10 (OOD)**:\nGSM8K: RASP 70 vs Std 53 (+17). MATH-500: RASP 55 vs Std 42 (+13). HumanEval: RASP 40 vs Std 33 (+7).\n\n**Table 11 (Structured vs Unstructured)**:\nRASP structured: 70%, 1.28× speedup. Unstructured: 60%, 1.02× speedup.\n\n**Table 12 (Extended baselines)**: All methods at 20% with GRPO Δ pattern: RASP(+13) >> GRASPrune(+3) > Bonsai(−3) > Standard(−11). Random and Unstructured(+5) also shown.\n\n**Table 13 (Collapse map)**: 20-25% recoverable, 30% near-collapse, 35% collapsed.\n\n**Section 5.12 (CKA reliability)**: Split-half ρ=0.93. Cal size: 256→29%, 512→31%, 1024→32%. Pruned-set overlap 86-95%.\n\n**Inference (Appendix C)**: 9.7ms/tok (1.28× speedup), 18.5% memory reduction.\n\n## Experiment Design Coverage\n\nThe full experiment design has 18 planned experiments. All 18 are represented in the paper:\n- plan_001-004: Core pipeline (Tables 1, 3)\n- plan_005: GRASPrune (Tables 1, 8)\n- plan_006: Multi-seed (Table 2)\n- plan_007: Scoring ablation (Table 6)\n- plan_008: CKA-recoverability regression (Table 5)\n- plan_009: 2×2 interaction (Table 4)\n- plan_010-011: Multi-sparsity (Table 9)\n- plan_012: OOD eval (Table 10)\n- plan_013: Bonsai (Tables 1, 12)\n- plan_014: CKA reliability (Section 5.12)\n- plan_015: α ablation (Table 7)\n- plan_016: Inference benchmark (Table 11, Appendix C)\n- plan_017: Unstructured baseline (Tables 11, 12)\n- plan_018: SFT OOD eval (Section 5.10)\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (methods + experiments, numbered)\n3. **Weaknesses** (methods + experiments, numbered, with severity: Major/Minor)\n4. **Questions to Authors** (numbered)\n5. **Missing Experiments or Controls** (if any from the 18-experiment design are inadequately reported)\n6. **Statistical Rigor Assessment**\n7. **Reproducibility Concerns**\n8. **Overall Score** (1-6) with justification\n9. **Confidence** (1-5, where 5 = certain)\n\nBe rigorous. A top venue expects: multiple models/datasets, strong baselines, proper statistical testing, ablations, and clear methodology. Evaluate whether the claims are adequately supported by the evidence presented.","agentId":"a853eb05b34e76496","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me now produce the formal review, carefully distinguishing between what has been actually run versus what is \"imagined\" (dream) data.\n\nBased on my thorough analysis of the paper materials, project logs, decision history, and experiment status, here is my review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning for Reasoning Language Models\n\n**Reviewer R1 — Focus: Methods Soundness & Experimental Rigor**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper proposes RASP, a structured pruning method that integrates intra-layer CKA-based functional overlap into the pruning score to optimize for post-RLVR recoverability rather than immediate post-pruning accuracy. The scoring function S(h) = I(h) x (1-F(h))^alpha penalizes redundant structures, making them candidates for removal under the hypothesis that RLVR can redistribute their function. On DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B at 20% sparsity, the paper claims a 17pp post-GRPO advantage over standard pruning (70% vs 53% GSM8K), a significant pruning-by-recovery interaction (25pp, p=0.0008), and that CKA overlap is the strongest predictor of per-structure recoverability (beta=0.38, partial R^2=0.22).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n**S1. Novel and well-motivated research question.** The idea that pruning and recovery are coupled optimization problems is genuinely interesting. The \"Redundancy Paradox\" framing --- that redundancy is not merely expendable but is the resource RLVR exploits --- is intellectually compelling and, if validated, would change how the community thinks about pruning for reasoning models.\n\n**S2. Well-designed 2x2 factorial analysis (Claim 3).** The pruning x recovery interaction design (Table 4) is the strongest conceptual contribution. If the 25pp interaction holds (main effects non-significant, interaction significant at p=0.0008), this is a clean and convincing demonstration that optimal pruning depends on recovery paradigm. This is the kind of analysis that elevates a paper above \"yet another pruning metric.\"\n\n**S3. Comprehensive experiment design covering 18 experiments.** The planned experiment matrix is thorough: multiple baselines (Standard, GRASPrune, Bonsai, Random, Unstructured), multi-sparsity (20/25/30%), OOD evaluation (MATH-500, HumanEval), scoring ablation, alpha sensitivity, multi-seed robustness, CKA reliability, inference benchmarks. If all were faithfully executed, this would be unusually thorough for the pruning literature.\n\n**S4. GRASPrune as a confound-breaking baseline (Table 8).** The inclusion of GRASPrune --- which has low post-prune accuracy (10%) but positive GRPO delta (+3pp) while Standard has higher post-prune accuracy (27%) but negative GRPO delta (-11pp) --- breaks the naive \"recovery is just a function of post-prune accuracy\" confound. This is a well-chosen control.\n\n**S5. Mixed-effects regression with appropriate random effects (Table 5).** Using layer as a random effect for the N=784 MLP group regression is methodologically appropriate. Reporting marginal R^2, conditional R^2, ICC, VIF, and controlling for competing predictors (weight norm, gradient importance, activation magnitude) shows statistical maturity.\n\n**S6. Honest scope limitation of CKA claims to MLP groups.** The paper correctly notes that at 20-25% sparsity, 0/112 attention GQA groups are pruned, and Claim 2's scope is limited to MLP channel groups. This transparency is appreciated.\n\n**S7. Thorough CKA reliability analysis.** Split-half rho=0.93, calibration size sensitivity (3pp range across 256/512/1024), and pruned-set overlap (86-95%) provide evidence that the CKA scoring is a stable measurement, not an artifact of a particular calibration sample.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1 [Major]. The GRPO results --- the entire evidential basis for Claims 1 and 3 --- appear to be imagined/projected, not actually obtained.** This is the single most critical issue. The project's internal records (ROADMAP.md, decisions.yaml, report.md) reveal that as of the paper submission date (2026-05-15), GRPO v8 is still running (ETA 2026-05-16 11:30 UTC). Versions v1 through v7 all failed due to engineering issues (truncation, collapse, OOM, disk full, silent death). The actual paper LaTeX (experiments.tex) contains \"[TBD: GRPO results]\" placeholders throughout. The \"dream report\" (best-case scenario) produces the numbers reported in the paper summary (70% vs 53%, interaction 25pp, etc.), but these are explicitly labeled as \"imagined_experiments\" in the project files. The actual confirmed results are only: post-prune RASP 31% vs Standard 27% (4pp, initially non-significant at N=100), post-SFT Standard 64% > RASP 57%. Every number in Tables 1, 3, 4, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12 that involves GRPO is projected. If GRPO v8 fails (as v1-v7 did), the paper has no viable Claim 1 or Claim 3.\n\n**W2 [Major]. Single model, single architecture.** All experiments use DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B exclusively. This is a distilled 7B model from one family (Qwen2.5). There is no validation on any other model (LLaMA, Mistral, Gemma), any other scale (1B, 14B, 32B), or even the non-distilled version of the same model. The \"Redundancy Paradox\" is stated as a general principle about RLVR recovery, but evidence comes from one model at one scale. For a top venue, this is insufficient to support the generality of the framing.\n\n**W3 [Major]. Evaluation is overwhelmingly GSM8K-centric.** GSM8K is used for calibration, SFT training, GRPO training, and primary evaluation. The model is trained and evaluated on the same benchmark distribution. OOD evaluation (MATH-500, HumanEval) is reported but: (a) the GRPO-based numbers for OOD appear to be imagined (same concern as W1), and (b) only post-pruning OOD is actually testable without GRPO completion. The recovery pipeline is GSM8K-specific (SFT on GSM8K train, GRPO on GSM8K train), raising the question of whether RASP's advantage is specific to GSM8K-style arithmetic reasoning.\n\n**W4 [Major]. The alpha ablation reveals a troubling pattern (Table 7).** The monotonically increasing curve (alpha=0: 25%, alpha=0.25: 27%, alpha=0.5: 29%, alpha=1.0: 31%, alpha=2.0: 35%) with no peak strongly suggests that the (1-F(h))^alpha term is simply acting as a regularizer that spreads pruning more evenly across layers, not specifically that CKA overlap captures \"functional redundancy\" for RLVR. Any monotonic reweighting that disperses pruning would produce a similar curve. The absence of a non-monotonic relationship (i.e., a point where too much CKA emphasis hurts) weakens the mechanistic interpretation. The paper needs to control for this confound --- e.g., by comparing against a random dispersion score (1-R(h))^alpha where R(h) is random or layer-dependent but not CKA.\n\n**W5 [Major]. Potential circularity in the CKA-recoverability regression (Claim 2).** The regression in Table 5 predicts \"per-structure recoverability\" from CKA overlap. But recoverability is defined as post-recovery accuracy, and RASP itself uses CKA to decide which structures to prune. If the pruning decision is CKA-driven, and the dependent variable is accuracy after pruning a CKA-selected set, the predictor and outcome are mechanically coupled. The paper needs to establish this correlation on models pruned by a CKA-independent method (e.g., Standard-pruned or randomly-pruned models) to demonstrate that CKA overlap predicts recoverability independently of the pruning criterion.\n\n**W6 [Minor]. The per-layer capacity floor (40%) is a significant design choice that is not ablated.** The paper mentions this floor was added because \"unconstrained global ranking at 20% sparsity concentrates pruning in layers with uniformly high functional overlap, collapsing those layers' capacity.\" This floor may be doing much of the heavy lifting --- RASP might primarily work by preventing layer collapse rather than through CKA-based selection per se. The paper mentions a planned ablation over tau in {0%, 20%, 40%, 60%} but this appears as \"[TBD]\" in the experiments section.\n\n**W7 [Minor]. Multi-seed analysis only covers post-prune, not the full pipeline.** Table 2 reports 5-seed variance for post-prune accuracy (4.5 +/- 0.8 pp gap, p=0.0002), but the critical claim is about post-GRPO recovery. GRPO training is stochastic and adds substantial variance. A 5-seed analysis of the full pipeline (prune -> SFT -> GRPO -> eval) would be far more convincing but is absent.\n\n**W8 [Minor]. Post-prune accuracy gap is small and originally mischaracterized.** The project history reveals that the original \"RASP 29% vs Standard 1%\" result (a 29x advantage) was due to an eval bug (max_new_tokens=10 truncation). After correction, the gap is 31% vs 27% (4pp), not significant at N=100. The paper's introduction still contains the erroneous \"29% vs 1%\" claim (line 28 of introduction.tex). While the corrected value appears in the dream paper, the actual LaTeX being prepared contains unresolved contradictions.\n\n**W9 [Minor]. GRPO hyperparameter sensitivity not explored.** The paper reports one GRPO configuration (beta=0.01, temp=0.4, rep_penalty=1.15, 100 steps). But the project logs show that GRPO behavior was extremely fragile: v1-v7 failed with different hyperparameter combinations. A natural question is whether Standard-pruned models might recover under different GRPO settings (e.g., lower temperature, more steps, different learning rate). The comparison may be sensitive to these choices.\n\n**W10 [Minor]. The KL divergence argument (Table 3) is post-hoc.** The paper argues that RASP's higher KL (4.1 vs 1.8) indicates \"greater policy movement\" and active exploration. But higher KL could equally indicate instability or catastrophic forgetting. Without decomposing what the KL change represents (which reasoning paths were compressed vs. expanded), this is an unfalsifiable narrative.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Questions to Authors\n\n**Q1.** Can you confirm which results in the paper are from actually completed experiments versus projected/imagined numbers? Specifically, have GRPO recovery experiments been completed for any configuration?\n\n**Q2.** The alpha ablation shows a monotonically increasing curve. Have you tested whether a control score of the form S(h) = I(h) x (1-D(h))^alpha, where D(h) is a dispersion term unrelated to CKA (e.g., inverse layer concentration), produces a similar monotonic improvement? This would distinguish the \"CKA captures functional redundancy\" story from the \"any dispersive reweighting helps\" story.\n\n**Q3.** For the mixed-effects regression (Table 5), was the recoverability dependent variable measured on RASP-pruned models, Standard-pruned models, or both? If only on RASP-pruned models, how do you address the circularity concern (predictor and selection criterion are the same)?\n\n**Q4.** The per-layer capacity floor of 40% was introduced to fix a collapse failure mode. What is the post-prune accuracy of RASP without this floor? If the floor alone accounts for most of the 4pp post-prune advantage, the contribution of CKA overlap scoring is marginal.\n\n**Q5.** Why was alpha=1.0 selected as the default when alpha=2.0 performs better (35% vs 31%)? If the answer is \"conservative choice,\" this undermines the paper's own claim that CKA emphasis is beneficial.\n\n**Q6.** The paper argues that Standard pruning \"accidentally removes functionally unique structures.\" Can you quantify this? How many of the 200 structures removed by Standard vs RASP are classified as \"functionally unique\" (e.g., bottom quartile of F(h))?\n\n**Q7.** You report that Standard achieves 64% post-SFT versus RASP's 57%. Is this 7pp gap significant? If so, an alternative interpretation is that Standard preserves a better architecture for learning and GRPO simply fails due to hyperparameter fragility rather than structural composition.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Missing Experiments or Controls\n\n**E1.** The capacity floor ablation (tau in {0%, 20%, 40%, 60%}) is listed as TBD. This is essential to isolate the CKA contribution from the floor contribution.\n\n**E2.** A dispersion control baseline (non-CKA-based reweighting that prevents layer concentration) is needed to test whether CKA overlap specifically, or any dispersive penalty, drives the improvement.\n\n**E3.** Multi-seed evaluation of the full pipeline (including GRPO) is missing. Post-prune multi-seed is necessary but not sufficient.\n\n**E4.** A second model family (e.g., LLaMA-3.1-8B with reasoning recovery, or Mistral-7B-Instruct) would substantially strengthen generalization claims.\n\n**E5.** Correlation between I(h) and F(h) is not reported. If high-importance structures tend to have high overlap, the (1-F)^alpha term may be approximately equivalent to a different importance normalization, not a fundamentally new signal.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Statistical Rigor Assessment\n\nThe paper demonstrates awareness of statistical methodology (mixed-effects regression, bootstrap CIs, paired t-tests, Fisher's exact test, Cohen's h). The regression design in Table 5 is appropriate if the circularity concern (W5) is addressed. The 2x2 interaction test is well-specified.\n\nHowever, there are significant gaps:\n\n- The primary claim (17pp GRPO gap) lacks any significance test in the actual paper --- bootstrap CIs are reported only in the dream version.\n- N=100 quick evaluations are used for several comparisons where N=1319 is needed (the paper acknowledges this).\n- The alpha ablation lacks significance tests between adjacent values. With N=100 and overlapping CIs, most pairwise differences (e.g., 29% vs 31%) are non-significant.\n- The interaction ANOVA (Table 4) is a 2x2 design with N=4 cell-level observations (one model per cell). The degrees of freedom for the interaction test are not reported. If this is an observation-level test (comparing N=1319 predictions per cell), the independence assumption is violated since all predictions come from the same model. If it is a model-level test, N=4 is inadequate.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Reproducibility Concerns\n\n**R1.** The codebase exists and appears functional (cka_overlap.py, rasp_scoring.py, prune.py, train_grpo.py, eval_gsm8k.py). Core algorithmic components are reproducible.\n\n**R2.** The GRPO recovery stage is extremely fragile. Eight versions were attempted, seven failed for engineering reasons. Reproducing the GRPO recovery results on different hardware would likely require substantial hyperparameter tuning not documented in the paper.\n\n**R3.** The per-layer capacity floor (40%) was introduced to fix a specific failure mode. The paper presents it as a minor engineering choice, but the project logs suggest it is critical for avoiding catastrophic collapse. Its interaction with the CKA scoring is not characterized.\n\n**R4.** The calibration sensitivity (D022) reveals that the model is at a \"fragile threshold\" at 20% sparsity, where small changes in calibration samples (256 vs 512) cause large accuracy swings. This fragility makes reproduction on different calibration sets potentially unstable.\n\n**R5.** Hyperparameters for GRPO evolved through 8 iterations of failure-driven tuning. The final configuration (v8: 8-bit AdamW, max_completion_length=1024, beta=0.01, temp=0.4, rep_penalty=1.15, format_reward=0.1, gradient_checkpointing=True) was arrived at through extensive debugging, not principled selection. This makes the comparison with Standard potentially unfair if Standard requires a different GRPO configuration.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Overall Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n\n**Justification.** The research question is genuinely novel and the experimental design (if fully executed) would be strong. The \"Redundancy Paradox\" framing and the 2x2 pruning-by-recovery interaction are conceptual contributions that would interest the community. However, several issues prevent acceptance:\n\n1. The headline results (Tables 1, 3, 4, and most others involving GRPO) appear to be projected rather than obtained. The core experiments were still running at submission time, with 7/8 prior attempts having failed. For a top venue, results must be actual, not imagined.\n\n2. Single model/single architecture severely limits the generalizability of claims stated as general principles about RLVR and structured pruning.\n\n3. The alpha ablation's monotonic pattern raises a serious confound: CKA may be acting as a generic dispersion regularizer rather than capturing \"functional redundancy\" per se. Without a dispersion control, the mechanistic narrative is underdetermined.\n\n4. The circularity concern in Claim 2 (CKA predicts recoverability when CKA is used to select pruning targets) is not adequately addressed.\n\nIf the GRPO results are genuine and the authors can address the dispersion confound and circularity concerns, this paper could merit a higher score (4-5). As submitted, the evidentiary basis is insufficient.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Confidence: 4/5\n\nI have carefully examined the paper text, project files, experiment logs, decision history, and codebase. I am confident in my assessment of what has been actually verified versus projected. The one uncertainty is whether GRPO v8 completed successfully between my review and the final submission --- if it did and the numbers match projections, the severity of W1 decreases, though the remaining weaknesses (W2-W5) stand independently."}],"totalDurationMs":172765,"totalTokens":110903,"totalToolUseCount":27,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":31916,"cache_read_input_tokens":74429,"output_tokens":4557,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":31916},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":4557,"cache_read_input_tokens":74429,"cache_creation_input_tokens":31916,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":31916,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":16,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":8}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a818c001c8d855eef","tool_use_id":"toolu_01XRksUD5v4kDZQfKPL7aJke","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":88281,"tool_uses":26,"duration_ms":180771},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"6f263d2b-36ec-43fd-b69b-76a0e6b2b6ad","session_id":"f3e56fa7-bcae-41e7-8b96-7c90222ffe81"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01W6Zx4oSjU44tBHPTFfo6Y2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SnSkg1qAFAzvFs3KpnTQ14","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Reviewer R2 Report: Novelty, Related Work, Conceptual Contribution, Writing Quality\n\n**Paper:** Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning: Optimizing What to Remove for How You Recover\n**Reviewer Focus:** Novelty, Related Work, Conceptual Contribution, Writing Quality\n\n---\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper proposes RASP, a structured pruning method that accounts for downstream RLVR-based recovery by incorporating intra-layer CKA functional overlap into the pruning score: S(h) = I(h) x (1-F(h))^alpha. The central claim is the \"Redundancy Paradox\" -- structures with high functional overlap, traditionally considered expendable, are precisely those whose function RLVR can redistribute, making them safely removable while functionally unique structures must be preserved. Evaluated on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B at 20% sparsity, the paper reports an 84.1% recovery rate for RASP vs 63.7% for standard pruning, a significant pruning x recovery interaction (25pp, p=0.0008), and CKA overlap as the dominant predictor of recoverability (beta=0.38, partial R^2=0.22).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Novelty Assessment\n\n**What is genuinely new:**\n- The explicit framing of pruning as a decision that should be optimized for a specific recovery paradigm (RLVR vs SFT) is novel. No prior work in the structured pruning or model recovery literature poses the question this way.\n- The adaptation of CKA from inter-layer depth pruning (MPruner, Lachance) to intra-layer width pruning for predicting per-structure recoverability under RLVR is a meaningful methodological extension.\n- The empirical observation that GRPO reverses the SFT-stage ranking (Standard > RASP under SFT, RASP > Standard under GRPO) -- if robustly verified -- is a striking finding with implications beyond pruning.\n\n**What is incremental:**\n- The scoring formula S(h) = I(h) x (1-F(h))^alpha is a single multiplicative re-weighting of an existing importance score. Technically this is a one-line modification to any Taylor-importance pruner. The novelty is conceptual rather than algorithmic.\n- The two-stage recovery pipeline (SFT warmup then GRPO) follows Self-Healing (2025) directly and is not a contribution of this paper.\n- CKA itself is a well-established metric (Kornblith et al., 2019). Using it for within-layer pairwise similarity is straightforward once the idea is stated.\n\n**Assessment:** The conceptual contribution -- \"optimize pruning for recovery, not just for immediate accuracy\" -- is the paper's real value, and it is a worthwhile reframing of the problem. The method is technically simple, which is not inherently a weakness if the empirical evidence is decisive. However, the gap between the strength of the conceptual framing (Redundancy Paradox, paradigm-conditioned trainability, LTH generalization) and the simplicity of the actual method (one multiplicative term) creates a mismatch that weakens the paper. The contribution is best characterized as \"a strong empirical finding wrapped in a slightly overwrought conceptual framework.\"\n\n---\n\n## 3. Related Work Completeness\n\n**Well-handled:**\n- Self-Healing (Chen et al., 2025): correctly identified as the closest predecessor, with a clean differentiation (they use standard pruning without questioning the pruning decision).\n- MPruner (Liu et al., 2024): correctly positioned as inter-layer depth pruning with CKA vs RASP's intra-layer width pruning.\n- GRASPrune (Wang et al., 2026): appropriate as primary pruning baseline.\n- \"RL Squeezes, SFT Expands\" (Huang et al., 2026): good theoretical anchor.\n\n**Missing or under-discussed (severity in brackets):**\n\n1. **[Major] FANG (Yu et al., Dec 2025)** -- Function-Aware Neuron Grouping. This is the closest conceptual competitor: it groups FFN neurons by functional roles via activation clustering and retains functionally critical shared neurons. Both FANG and RASP share the motivation of \"identify functionally critical structures and preserve them during pruning.\" The paper's literature map marks this as must-cite, and the revised related_work.tex does include it, but the dream summary's Related Work section omits it. If the final paper discusses FANG, the differentiation must be sharp: FANG uses activation-based grouping for immediate post-prune accuracy; RASP uses CKA for post-recovery accuracy. Without this discussion, a knowledgeable reviewer will immediately flag the gap.\n\n2. **[Major] FuncNet (Liu et al., Aug 2025)** -- ICA-based functional network identification for pruning. Same motivation (treat LLM as having functional subnetworks, preserve the important ones). Must be discussed.\n\n3. **[Moderate] Davari et al. (ICLR 2023)** -- \"Reliability of CKA as a Similarity Measure.\" Demonstrates that CKA is sensitive to outliers and can be manipulated without changing functional behavior. The paper uses CKA as a central metric but never discusses its known failure modes. This is needed for honest methodology.\n\n4. **[Moderate] RestoreLCC (Feng et al., NeurIPS 2025 Spotlight)** -- Plug-and-play recovery via lost component compensation. An alternative recovery paradigm that should be cited to contextualize the recovery design space.\n\n5. **[Moderate] Boiling Frog (Zhao et al., Feb 2026)** -- Gradual prune-tune loop. Similar prune-then-recover paradigm but uses iterative SFT. The revised related_work.tex includes it, but it should appear in the final paper.\n\n6. **[Minor] \"From LLMs to LRMs\" (Ding et al., Jan 2026)** -- Systematically compares width vs depth pruning for reasoning models, finding width pruning preserves reasoning better. Validates the paper's focus on width pruning but not cited in the summary's related work.\n\n7. **[Minor] The reference list as described in the summary (~15 citations) is thin for a paper at this intersection. The literature map identifies 35+ relevant papers. A target of 25+ would be appropriate for a top venue.\n\n**Positioning accuracy:** The differentiation from MPruner (inter-layer vs intra-layer) and Self-Healing (standard pruning vs recovery-aware pruning) is accurate and crisp. The LTH connection is illustrative but shallow -- it adds narrative color without substance.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Strengths\n\n1. **Genuine gap identified.** The observation that pruning and recovery are treated as independent, and that they should be coupled, is correct and important. This is a real contribution to how the community thinks about model compression for reasoning models.\n\n2. **Clean experimental design for the interaction effect.** The 2x2 design (RASP vs Standard) x (SFT-only vs SFT+GRPO) with the 25pp interaction effect (p=0.0008) is well-structured for demonstrating that the recovery method conditions the optimal pruning strategy. This is the paper's strongest piece of evidence.\n\n3. **GRASPrune as a confound-breaking baseline.** Including GRASPrune (which has different post-prune accuracy than Standard but still shows positive GRPO gain) helps disentangle \"better pruning\" from \"better recovery substrate.\" This was a smart experimental choice.\n\n4. **Rigorous statistical methodology.** Mixed-effects regression with layer as random effect, proper significance testing, bootstrap CIs, multi-seed robustness (5 seeds) -- this exceeds the statistical standard of most ML papers.\n\n5. **Honest limitations section.** Acknowledging single model, single benchmark, no inference speedup measurement, and limited seed diversity is appropriate and avoids overclaiming.\n\n6. **Striking observation that Standard LOSES from GRPO.** The finding that importance-only pruned models degrade from 64% to 53% under GRPO (while RASP gains from 57% to 70%) is counterintuitive and attention-grabbing. If robust, this changes how practitioners should think about compression pipelines.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. [Major] The \"Redundancy Paradox\" is oversold and potentially mischaracterized.**\nThe paper frames functional redundancy as a \"paradox\" -- that redundancy, usually seen as expendability, actually signals recoverability. But this is not paradoxical in any formal sense. Redundancy enabling graceful degradation and recovery is a basic principle in fault-tolerant systems, information theory, and even biology. Calling it a \"paradox\" implies a logical contradiction where there is merely a non-obvious design trade-off. \"Redundancy Inversion\" or \"Recovery-Redundancy Trade-off\" would be more precise. The current framing inflates the conceptual contribution.\n\n**W2. [Major] Method is technically thin for a top venue.**\nThe entire method is a single multiplicative re-weighting: S(h) = I(h) x (1-F(h))^alpha. This is a one-line code change to any importance-based pruner. The paper compensates with extensive experiments, but the methodological contribution itself -- \"multiply importance by a CKA-derived penalty\" -- is something a competent practitioner would try as a first experiment once the hypothesis is stated. For a top venue, either the method should be deeper (e.g., learned combination, theoretical analysis of why this functional form is appropriate) or the empirical story must be airtight (which it nearly is, but see W3-W5).\n\n**W3. [Major] Causal story for the interaction effect is speculative.**\nThe paper invokes \"RL Squeezes, SFT Expands\" (Huang et al., 2026) to explain why RASP benefits from GRPO while Standard degrades. But the connection is narrative, not mechanistic. The paper provides no direct evidence that GRPO actually \"redistributes function\" from pruned high-overlap structures to retained structures. No attention pattern analysis, no representation trajectory analysis, no gradient flow analysis during GRPO training. The causal chain (CKA overlap -> RLVR redistribution -> recovery) remains a plausible hypothesis, not a demonstrated mechanism.\n\n**W4. [Major] Correlation between I(h) and F(h) not reported.**\nIf importance I(h) and overlap F(h) are highly correlated, the multiplicative formula is dominated by one factor, and the \"both matter\" narrative of the method is misleading. The paper must report this correlation. From the memory notes, MLP groups have F(h) in [0.879, 0.997], which is an extremely compressed range -- meaning (1-F(h))^alpha operates on a very narrow interval. If the effective discrimination is driven almost entirely by I(h) with F(h) acting as a minor re-ranking perturbation, the conceptual framing overstates CKA's role.\n\n**W5. [Moderate] Single model evaluation limits generalizability of conceptual claims.**\nThe Redundancy Paradox is framed as a general principle about structured pruning and RLVR recovery. But all evidence comes from one model (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B) on one primary benchmark (GSM8K) at one primary sparsity (20%). The OOD evaluations (MATH-500, HumanEval) help, but a second model family (e.g., LLaMA-based, Mistral-based) is needed to support the generality of the claims. Without it, this could be an artifact of Qwen's specific architecture or the distillation procedure.\n\n**W6. [Moderate] At 20% sparsity, only MLP groups are pruned (0/112 attention GQA groups).**\nThis means RASP's claimed ability to handle \"attention heads or MLP channels\" jointly is untested at this sparsity. The framework scores both types but only MLP groups are actually affected. The paper should be more upfront about this -- the method is presented as general but evaluated only on MLP channel group selection. The CKA overlap for attention groups is computed but never influences any pruning decision.\n\n**W7. [Moderate] alpha hyperparameter analysis is presented but lacks post-GRPO results.**\nThe summary mentions alpha \"monotonically increasing from 25% (alpha=0) to 35% (alpha=2), 10pp range\" for post-prune accuracy. But the alpha ablation that matters is post-GRPO accuracy across alpha values. If GRPO recovery is insensitive to alpha, the CKA term adds complexity without proportional benefit for the full pipeline.\n\n**W8. [Minor] The LTH/paradigm-conditioned trainability connection is superficial.**\nInvoking the Lottery Ticket Hypothesis adds perceived depth but no substance. LTH is about finding sparse subnetworks that train well from initialization; RASP is about choosing which dense structures to remove for recovery under a specific fine-tuning paradigm. The analogy is loose. This framing should be trimmed to a single sentence or removed.\n\n**W9. [Minor] Writing could be more concise.**\nThe Results section (3.5 pages, 12 subsections, 11 tables) is disproportionately long relative to the 1-page Method section. Some of the 12 subsections could be consolidated. The paper's story would benefit from tighter organization: lead with the interaction effect (the strongest finding), then support with ablations.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Conceptual Concerns\n\n**Is the \"Redundancy Paradox\" well-founded?**\n\nPartially. The empirical observation -- that RASP-pruned models respond better to GRPO than Standard-pruned models -- is real and interesting. But the causal explanation offered (functional overlap enables RLVR redistribution) is under-supported. Several alternative explanations exist:\n\n1. **Starting accuracy threshold.** RASP starts at 31% post-prune vs Standard at 27%. After SFT, Standard catches up (64% vs 57%). The GRPO divergence could reflect a non-linearity in GRPO's response to the *type* of errors remaining after SFT, not the structural composition of the pruned model. The paper does not disentangle these.\n\n2. **Diversity preservation.** RASP protects functionally unique structures. This means the retained architecture has higher representational diversity. GRPO, being an RL method that relies on exploration, benefits from diverse representations. This is a simpler explanation than \"functional redistribution\" and does not require CKA specifically -- any diversity-preserving metric might work. The paper does not test alternatives.\n\n3. **Standard degradation under GRPO.** The claim that Standard LOSES 11pp from GRPO (64 -> 53) is very unusual. This could indicate that Standard-pruned models are in a pathological region of the loss landscape where GRPO exploration is actively destructive -- a finding about GRPO fragility rather than about pruning strategy. This deserves investigation as an alternative explanation.\n\n**Is the causal story convincing?**\n\nNo. The paper provides correlational evidence (CKA overlap correlates with recoverability) and a plausible narrative (RLVR redistributes function of high-overlap structures). But it does not provide mechanistic evidence. What would be convincing: (a) showing that retained structures actually increase their CKA similarity to pruned structures' roles after GRPO, (b) showing that GRPO gradient flow preferentially flows through high-overlap retained structures, or (c) an intervention study where you artificially reduce overlap in RASP-pruned models and show GRPO recovery degrades.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Writing Quality\n\n**Clarity:** Generally good. The three-stage framework (analyze, score, recover) is clearly explained. The scoring formula is simple and well-motivated. The distinction from standard pruning (alpha=0) is clean.\n\n**Organization:** The 12-subsection Results section is excessive. The paper would benefit from consolidating to 5-6 subsections: Main Results, Interaction Effect, Per-Structure Analysis, Alpha Ablation, Robustness. The current structure reads like a lab notebook rather than a crafted narrative.\n\n**Notation:** Consistent and appropriate. The use of I(h), F(h), S(h) is intuitive.\n\n**Length:** The 3.5-page Results section and 0.5-page Related Work create an imbalance. Related Work should be expanded (see Section 3 above) and Results should be tightened.\n\n**Appropriate scope language:** The Limitations section is well-written and honest. However, the Abstract and Introduction make claims (\"establishes that pruning and recovery are coupled optimization problems\") that are stronger than the single-model evidence supports. Recommended softening: \"provides evidence that\" rather than \"establishes.\"\n\n---\n\n## 8. Questions to Authors\n\nQ1. What is the Pearson/Spearman correlation between I(h) and F(h) for MLP groups? Given F(h) in [0.879, 0.997], what is the effective dynamic range of (1-F(h))^alpha, and how many rank changes does the CKA term actually produce relative to importance-only ranking?\n\nQ2. Can you provide a mechanistic analysis of what happens during GRPO recovery in RASP-pruned vs Standard-pruned models? Specifically: do retained structures' representations shift to compensate for pruned structures' functions?\n\nQ3. Why does Standard-pruned model DEGRADE under GRPO (64% -> 53%)? Is this a general phenomenon or specific to this configuration? Does it persist across seeds?\n\nQ4. Have you tested alternative overlap metrics (SVCCA, mutual information, simple cosine similarity of mean activations) in place of CKA? Given the narrow F(h) range [0.879, 0.997], a metric with better discrimination in this regime might outperform CKA.\n\nQ5. At what sparsity level do attention GQA groups begin to be pruned, and does RASP's advantage persist when both MLP and attention groups are affected?\n\nQ6. The post-prune gap is 4pp (non-significant at p=0.54), but the post-GRPO gap is 17pp. Can you characterize what happens during the SFT stage that narrows the post-prune gap and what happens during GRPO that widens it? Training dynamics curves would be informative.\n\nQ7. How sensitive is the result to the per-layer capacity floor (40%)? This constraint may be doing significant work -- what happens at 20% or 60%?\n\n---\n\n## 9. Overall Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept)\n\n**Justification:**\n\nThe paper identifies a genuine and important gap: no prior work asks whether the pruning decision should be optimized for the recovery paradigm. This is a meaningful conceptual contribution, and the question will remain relevant as compression of reasoning LLMs becomes standard practice. The experimental design -- particularly the 2x2 interaction test and the GRASPrune confound-breaking baseline -- is well-crafted, and the statistical methodology exceeds community norms.\n\nHowever, the paper has significant weaknesses that prevent a stronger recommendation:\n\n(a) The method is technically thin -- a single multiplicative term. This puts the entire burden of the contribution on the empirical results, which must be airtight. They are strong but not fully airtight: the causal story is speculative, the I(h)-F(h) correlation is unreported, and the single-model evaluation limits generalizability.\n\n(b) The conceptual framing (\"Redundancy Paradox,\" \"paradigm-conditioned trainability\") oversells what is ultimately a clean empirical finding. The paper would be stronger if it presented the finding plainly and let readers draw conceptual conclusions, rather than wrapping it in grand framing that invites skepticism.\n\n(c) The critical finding -- that Standard-pruned models degrade under GRPO -- is insufficiently analyzed. This is potentially the most important result in the paper and deserves deeper investigation as a standalone contribution.\n\nFor acceptance, the paper needs: (1) I(h)-F(h) correlation analysis, (2) at least one alternative overlap metric comparison, (3) sharper FANG/FuncNet differentiation, (4) toned-down conceptual framing, and (5) some mechanistic evidence for the redistribution hypothesis. Items (1)-(4) are straightforward; item (5) would elevate the paper to Accept territory.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Confidence: 4/5\n\nI am confident in my assessment of the novelty, related work positioning, and conceptual framing. I have reviewed the full paper draft, project decision logs, and literature map. The one area where my confidence is lower is the GRPO results, which are reported as final in the review prompt but were still running (v8) at the time of the paper's development. If the GRPO results differ substantially from the dream-tier numbers presented, the assessment would change accordingly.\n","project":"recovery_aware_struct_prune","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260515T140651","overall_score":"Weak Accept (4/6)"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":150,"cache_read_input_tokens":87459,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":150,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XRksUD5v4kDZQfKPL7aJke","session_id":"f3e56fa7-bcae-41e7-8b96-7c90222ffe81","uuid":"268723f3-d9a1-4b5e-9512-aa08061f2a85"}
